**What**
`Store::put_schema` serializes and commits whatever `SchemaDoc` it is handed.
It checks neither internal consistency (do all `Ref`s resolve? is the `Ref`
graph acyclic?) nor any relationship to the schema it is replacing.

**Where**
`crates/gix-store/src/store.rs:94-98`.

**Why it matters**
Both failure modes reproduce immediately.

Unusable schema, published successfully. With `broken.json` holding
`{"root":{"Ref":"Nope"},"defs":{}}`:

```
$ git store schema put broken -F broken.json
2e3eade5811521c33acb221bcc350149c9351018
$ git store schema show broken
broken
  Nope                       <- rendered as if it were a type
$ git store put broken b1 -F empty-object.json
Error: at $: schema ref "Nope" has no definition in the document
```

A `Ref` cycle (`{"root":{"Ref":"A"},"defs":{"A":{"Ref":"A"}}}`) also publishes
fine and only fails at first use, with a max-depth error. Because schema refs
are deliberately undeletable (`store.rs:232-234`), the kind is now permanently
carrying a broken version and the only escape is publishing a new one.

Silent incompatibility, also published successfully: changing `serves` from
`U32` to `String` succeeds with no warning. Afterwards two entities of the same
kind have genuinely different shapes, and `schema show` describes neither
accurately:

```
$ git store schema show recipe
  serves: string
$ git store get recipe carbonara | grep serves
  "serves": 6                <- a number, written under the old schema
$ git store get recipe cacio | grep serves
  "serves": "two-ish"
```

Reads survive because each commit pins its own schema - that part of the design
is genuinely good - but `kind` stops meaning "these values share a shape",
which is the one guarantee a published schema is supposed to buy. There is no
compatibility classification, no migration path, and nothing in
`docs/specification.adoc` addresses evolution at all: the spec covers
`schema.representation` and `schema.generation` but is silent on what a *second*
version of a schema may do.

**Options**
- Option A - validate at publish: every `Ref` resolves, the `Ref` graph is
  acyclic, depth is within `MAX_DEPTH`. Cheap, purely additive, no format
  change. Should happen regardless of what else is decided.
- Option B - classify the diff against the previous schema (compatible /
  additive / breaking) and require `--allow-breaking` past a certain class.
  Gives the author a real slide: schema evolution as a first-class,
  history-aware operation. Trade-off: needs a defined compatibility relation,
  which is genuine design work.
- Option C - treat an incompatible change as a *new kind* rather than a new
  version, so a kind is immutable by construction. Trade-off: pushes naming
  churn onto users (`recipe`, `recipe2`), which is why most systems do not.

**Recommendation**
Option A before the talk, unconditionally - it is small and removes an
embarrassing live failure. Option B is the more interesting answer and the one
a design-conscious audience will ask about; even a written-down compatibility
rule in the spec, with the check deferred, would be a big improvement over
silence. High confidence on A, high confidence that B needs at minimum a
documented position.